(515884, 50044, 7889, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by xcharrier:</b></div><hr/><i>Very neat shot.  Thats amazing that you were able to get the center duck to stay still while the other two moved.   I''m assuming thats how the shot was made.</i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Duck! LOL<br/> <br/></td>', 'Originally posted by xcharrier:Very neat shot.  Thats amazing that you were able to get the center duck to stay still while the other two moved.   I''m assuming thats how the shot was made.  Duck! LOL ', '2003-12-17 15:32:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(515919, 50194, 7889, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Natator:</b></div><hr/><i>I don''t understand that comment there banmorn.  This shot does not show a vagina, or even close to one.  I think you maybe need to look up the definition ;)</i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Yeh i was confused with banmorn''s comment!! This photo suffered in the voting because a lot of ''voters'', for some reason, mark down shots showing parts of the body. Another in this challenge - The Strawberry on the erect nipple - suffered as well. Sad because both shots are very tastefully done.<br/> <br/>Mike<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by Natator:I don''t understand that comment there banmorn.  This shot does not show a vagina, or even close to one.  I think you maybe need to look up the definition ;)  Yeh i was confused with banmorn''s comment!! This photo suffered in the voting because a lot of ''voters'', for some reason, mark down shots showing parts of the body. Another in this challenge - The Strawberry on the erect nipple - suffered as well. Sad because both shots are very tastefully done. Mike', '2003-12-17 15:55:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(515980, 49548, 5112, '<td valign="top">Biliana, congratulations on an excellent finish!  I was just trying to find you on my list of favourites to send you this message, and I noticed I''d forgotten to put you there.  So that''s corrected :))))<br/> <br/>What a good idea this picture is!  I thought it was the hood of a car.  I LOVE the silvery colours.<br/> <br/>Ursula</td>', 'Biliana, congratulations on an excellent finish!  I was just trying to find you on my list of favourites to send you this message, and I noticed I''d forgotten to put you there.  So that''s corrected :)))) What a good idea this picture is!  I thought it was the hood of a car.  I LOVE the silvery colours. Ursula', '2003-12-17 16:32:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(516066, 50107, 14620, '<td valign="top">Thank you to those who posted comments.  Every little bit helps!  If anyone would like to offer a little critical input, feel free...<br/> <br/>Thanks Again,<br/>Jen</td>', 'Thank you to those who posted comments.  Every little bit helps!  If anyone would like to offer a little critical input, feel free... Thanks Again,Jen', '2003-12-17 18:22:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(516229, 50070, 327, '<td valign="top">Gina, I don''t see my comment below. ? Odd thought for sure I did one..?<br/>I really liked this shot, very attractive tones and pose.<br/></td>', 'Gina, I don''t see my comment below. ? Odd thought for sure I did one..?I really liked this shot, very attractive tones and pose.', '2003-12-17 21:22:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(516315, 49505, 9073, '<td valign="top">This is a nice photo, reflected in a suitably nice score. There is a serene elegance here, and the theme of simplicity is well-supported.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, the elements are arranged very well - the right-hand trees, the top left sun reflection, the bottom, underscored.<br/> <br/>The colour range is a bit limited. The reflection could be a touch brighter and the silhouette darker. Or the trees could have some more colour to balance with the brightness more. Or the overall brightness level could be increased. But, for me, somehow the colour range needs something extra. <br/> <br/>You could try cropping just to show the top half, portrait.<br/> <br/>Overall, a nice photo, but nothing outstanding.</td>', 'This is a nice photo, reflected in a suitably nice score. There is a serene elegance here, and the theme of simplicity is well-supported. Compositionally, the elements are arranged very well - the right-hand trees, the top left sun reflection, the bottom, underscored. The colour range is a bit limited. The reflection could be a touch brighter and the silhouette darker. Or the trees could have some more colour to balance with the brightness more. Or the overall brightness level could be increased. But, for me, somehow the colour range needs something extra.  You could try cropping just to show the top half, portrait. Overall, a nice photo, but nothing outstanding.', '2003-12-17 22:08:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(516382, 49622, 14653, '<td valign="top">thank you for everyone that liked this photo. there has been a couple questions regarding the moon in this photograph. the moon was NOT added to this photo. i just happened to be there at the right time to catch it where i did.<br/> <br/>thank you again.<br/> <br/>Remington</td>', 'thank you for everyone that liked this photo. there has been a couple questions regarding the moon in this photograph. the moon was NOT added to this photo. i just happened to be there at the right time to catch it where i did. thank you again. Remington', '2003-12-17 23:12:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(516468, 50050, 14256, '<td valign="top">ova je odlicno pogodjena</td>', 'ova je odlicno pogodjena', '2003-12-18 02:00:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(516771, 49899, 6999, '<td valign="top"><b>from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Thing you achieve best of all here is the positioning of the tree within the framing of the shot: it bridges the transition from foreground to distance nicely. <br/> <br/>Focus and depth of field and all that technical stuff is pretty well done - though there seems to be a lack of detail: I wonder if it''s perhaps cropped slightly too far?<br/> <br/>The image suffers most, IMO, from an indecisiveness about what the true subject is: the tree? the peak and the openness of the view? the progression of hills into the distance? If it''s the tree, then I think it would need more careful placing against this background (obviously, by moving the photographer, not the tree itself): perhaps at least to exclude the town visible in the valley. There are too many small elements that compete for attention here for this not to suffer in voting in a simplicity challenge.<br/> <br/>Ed</td>', 'from the Critique Club  Thing you achieve best of all here is the positioning of the tree within the framing of the shot: it bridges the transition from foreground to distance nicely.  Focus and depth of field and all that technical stuff is pretty well done - though there seems to be a lack of detail: I wonder if it''s perhaps cropped slightly too far? The image suffers most, IMO, from an indecisiveness about what the true subject is: the tree? the peak and the openness of the view? the progression of hills into the distance? If it''s the tree, then I think it would need more careful placing against this background (obviously, by moving the photographer, not the tree itself): perhaps at least to exclude the town visible in the valley. There are too many small elements that compete for attention here for this not to suffer in voting in a simplicity challenge. Ed', '2003-12-18 11:35:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(516805, 49530, 14749, '<td valign="top">Thank you all for your comments.  I was experimenting with my camera and was trying to get a ''simple'' shot.  I know it isn''t a very interesting subject, but it was as simple as I could find.  I agree it is a little grainy, but I am just starting out and trying to get better.  That is how I learn, by trial and error.  Thanks again!</td>', 'Thank you all for your comments.  I was experimenting with my camera and was trying to get a ''simple'' shot.  I know it isn''t a very interesting subject, but it was as simple as I could find.  I agree it is a little grainy, but I am just starting out and trying to get better.  That is how I learn, by trial and error.  Thanks again!', '2003-12-18 12:00:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(516877, 50277, 6999, '<td valign="top">Well done Dan - the purity and cleanness of that blue is quite extraordinary: and you scored more tens than mine - not enough 9''s then?<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-12-18 13:25:24.</i></td>', 'Well done Dan - the purity and cleanness of that blue is quite extraordinary: and you scored more tens than mine - not enough 9''s then?', '2003-12-18 13:24:00.000000', '2003-12-18 13:25:24.000000', 0),
(516932, 49505, 9504, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Koriyama:</b></div><hr/><i>This is a nice photo, reflected in a suitably nice score. There is a serene elegance here, and the theme of simplicity is well-supported.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, the elements are arranged very well - the right-hand trees, the top left sun reflection, the bottom, underscored.<br/> <br/>The colour range is a bit limited. The reflection could be a touch brighter and the silhouette darker. Or the trees could have some more colour to balance with the brightness more. Or the overall brightness level could be increased. But, for me, somehow the colour range needs something extra. <br/> <br/>You could try cropping just to show the top half, portrait.<br/> <br/>Overall, a nice photo, but nothing outstanding.</i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I tend to be a realist and thought this was a unique scene...that''s what drew me in...  It''s not spectacular, but interesting.</td>', 'Originally posted by Koriyama:This is a nice photo, reflected in a suitably nice score. There is a serene elegance here, and the theme of simplicity is well-supported. Compositionally, the elements are arranged very well - the right-hand trees, the top left sun reflection, the bottom, underscored. The colour range is a bit limited. The reflection could be a touch brighter and the silhouette darker. Or the trees could have some more colour to balance with the brightness more. Or the overall brightness level could be increased. But, for me, somehow the colour range needs something extra.  You could try cropping just to show the top half, portrait. Overall, a nice photo, but nothing outstanding.  I tend to be a realist and thought this was a unique scene...that''s what drew me in...  It''s not spectacular, but interesting.', '2003-12-18 15:40:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(516942, 49779, 12332, '<td valign="top">I laughed when I saw this photo, and laughed a little more when I wrote my original comment.  But I was just cracking up big time reading all of the comments below.  Hilarious!  And you started it...how did your model keep a straight face?</td>', 'I laughed when I saw this photo, and laughed a little more when I wrote my original comment.  But I was just cracking up big time reading all of the comments below.  Hilarious!  And you started it...how did your model keep a straight face?', '2003-12-18 15:57:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(516961, 49779, 1031, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by wkmen:</b></div><hr/><i>I laughed when I saw this photo, and laughed a little more when I wrote my original comment.  But I was just cracking up big time reading all of the comments below.  Hilarious!  And you started it...how did your model keep a straight face?</i></td></tr></table> <br/>Ditto. Hope your GF has recovered ...</td>', 'Originally posted by wkmen:I laughed when I saw this photo, and laughed a little more when I wrote my original comment.  But I was just cracking up big time reading all of the comments below.  Hilarious!  And you started it...how did your model keep a straight face? Ditto. Hope your GF has recovered ...', '2003-12-18 17:02:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(516975, 49698, 5112, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Anne, hi, guess who got your picture :))<br/> <br/>First thing I''d like to say is that I absolutely love seashells.  This one is very beautiful, with the shiny parts and the hole to the left, and the little things on it at the right and top (don''t know what they''re called).  It also has a beautiful shape.<br/> <br/>That said, you probably know by now that this is not the best ever seashell picture that you''ll take :))  It looks like you placed it on a dark background?  The background looks almost like leather, but I can''t tell very well.  There are a couple sand grains at the front.  The light is very direct, looks straight on, and it bleaches out the shell.  Like a number of the commenters below, it might have worked better to use a more diffused or indirect light source, maybe from the side, maybe using a white cardboard as a reflector to the other side, something like that.  <br/> <br/>As a composition, the picture also is not quite there.  It is a shell placed in the middle of the picture, but there isn''t really much in the picture that will hold the attention of the viewer, or want her/him to come back and look at the picture some more.  Possibly photographing the shell in context, i.e. on the sand, close to water, or maybe not placed quite so in the middle of the picture.  I''m not sure.<br/> <br/>I''m also wondering about the colours.  They actually are beautiful, the light browns and pinks on the top part of the shell, the dark, dark gray background, the whites.  Yet, as a composition, since it is rather minimal, a b/w treatment might have worked here.  Again, I''m not sure, but it is something to try.<br/> <br/>I must say you picked a difficult subject for this challenge.  It fits it well (the challenge).  It''s a really interesting project, to try and figure out how you could take the most beautiful shell picture you can, one that people will say, "Wow!  Now that''s a shell!".  Oh well.  Just dreaming :)))<br/> <br/>Keep going, Anne, take care of yourself.<br/> <br/>Ursula</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club Anne, hi, guess who got your picture :)) First thing I''d like to say is that I absolutely love seashells.  This one is very beautiful, with the shiny parts and the hole to the left, and the little things on it at the right and top (don''t know what they''re called).  It also has a beautiful shape. That said, you probably know by now that this is not the best ever seashell picture that you''ll take :))  It looks like you placed it on a dark background?  The background looks almost like leather, but I can''t tell very well.  There are a couple sand grains at the front.  The light is very direct, looks straight on, and it bleaches out the shell.  Like a number of the commenters below, it might have worked better to use a more diffused or indirect light source, maybe from the side, maybe using a white cardboard as a reflector to the other side, something like that.   As a composition, the picture also is not quite there.  It is a shell placed in the middle of the picture, but there isn''t really much in the picture that will hold the attention of the viewer, or want her/him to come back and look at the picture some more.  Possibly photographing the shell in context, i.e. on the sand, close to water, or maybe not placed quite so in the middle of the picture.  I''m not sure. I''m also wondering about the colours.  They actually are beautiful, the light browns and pinks on the top part of the shell, the dark, dark gray background, the whites.  Yet, as a composition, since it is rather minimal, a b/w treatment might have worked here.  Again, I''m not sure, but it is something to try. I must say you picked a difficult subject for this challenge.  It fits it well (the challenge).  It''s a really interesting project, to try and figure out how you could take the most beautiful shell picture you can, one that people will say, "Wow!  Now that''s a shell!".  Oh well.  Just dreaming :))) Keep going, Anne, take care of yourself. Ursula', '2003-12-18 17:53:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(517120, 50278, 327, '<td valign="top">Really enjoyed this shot. I gave it a 9 only because I thought the background should of been all white. Sounds lame now that I type that remark. The colors, crop and perspective are awesome.</td>', 'Really enjoyed this shot. I gave it a 9 only because I thought the background should of been all white. Sounds lame now that I type that remark. The colors, crop and perspective are awesome.', '2003-12-18 21:07:59.000000', NULL, 0),
